//? Title: Ziggurat Sampling: Equal-Area Proposals and Gaussian Measurement Replicates
//? Subtitle: Deriving the fast path, checking the tails and simulating the eight schools
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The Ziggurat method makes most normal draws with a table lookup, a multiply
and a comparison. We derive its equal-area proposal rectangles, the wedge
acceptance test and exponential-tail rejection step. Actual path counts
separate fast proposals from successful returns, and Monte Carlo checks
cover both supported table sizes. The application uses
`dm::datasets::eight_schools()` directly: we perturb each reported estimate
by its standard error, then compare simulated positive fractions with
closed-form normal probabilities. These are conditional measurement
replicates; the sampler does not supply a hierarchical inference model.
)

\toc

> The data: eight estimates with different uncertainties

The eight-schools frame has eight rows and three columns: a school label,
an estimated coaching effect in test-score points, and its standard error.
It is small enough to inspect in full. Standard errors differ considerably,
so a single common noise scale would discard information.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("{}\n", dm::datasets::eight_schools().to_md());
}
```
