```{cpp, file="figures/ziggurat_schools.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto schools = dm::datasets::eight_schools();
  const auto y = schools.get_column("effect").values();
  const auto se = schools.get_column("std_error").values();
  std::vector<double> x(8), lo(8), hi(8);
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 8; ++i) { x[i] = i + 1; lo[i] = y[i] - se[i]; hi[i] = y[i] + se[i]; }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .segments(x, lo, x, hi, dm::RGB{96, 165, 250}, 2)
      .points(x, y, "reported estimate", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5)
      .abline_h(0, dm::RGB{100, 100, 100}, 1)
      .title("Eight schools: effects and measurement uncertainty")
      .x_label("school (1=A, ..., 8=H)").y_label("test-score points")
      .size(900, 460).save_svg("build/ziggurat_schools.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/ziggurat_schools.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Reported estimates with one-standard-error bars. These bars describe measurement uncertainty, rather than the Monte Carlo error of a simulation.)
\alttext(Eight estimated school effects plotted with vertical standard-error bars and a horizontal line at zero. Most bars overlap zero.)

> The method: a proposal envelope with equal areas

Symmetry lets us derive the algorithm on the positive half-line using the
unnormalised normal kernel \(f(x)=e^{-x^2/2}\). Normalisation cancels in
rejection ratios. Choose decreasing boundaries
\(x_1=r>x_2>\cdots>x_N=0\). Layer zero combines the rectangle under
height \(f(r)\) from zero to \(r\), with the remaining tail. Its area is

$$
V=rf(r)+\int_r^\infty f(t)\,dt
 =rf(r)+\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}\operatorname{erfc}\!\left(\frac{r}{\sqrt2}\right).
$$
\alttext(The common proposal area V equals r times f of r plus the positive normal-kernel tail integral beyond r.)

Represent this base proposal by an artificial width \(x_0=V/f(r)>r\).
Every other proposal is a rectangle of width \(x_i\) between heights
\(f(x_i)\) and \(f(x_{i+1})\). Equal area requires

$$
x_i\left[f(x_{i+1})-f(x_i)\right]=V,\qquad
x_{i+1}=\sqrt{-2\log\!\left(f(x_i)+\frac{V}{x_i}\right)}.
$$
\alttext(Rectangle i has area x i times the difference between its upper and lower heights. Solving for the next boundary gives the square root of minus two times the logarithm of f of x i plus V divided by x i.)

The last rectangle closes at the peak: \(x_{N-1}[1-f(x_{N-1})]=V\).
Together with the tail-area equation, this determines \(r\) for a chosen
\(N\). The implementation stores established constants for 128 and 256
layers; other layer counts are rejected, since reusing those constants
would no longer construct an equal-area cover.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  for (std::size_t n : {128, 256}) {
    const dm::algorithms::ZigguratNormal sampler(n);
    std::print("{} layers, r={:.12f}\n\n{}\n", n, sampler.tail_boundary(), sampler.table().head(5).to_md(8));
  }
}
```
