// result_begin:
// \image(figures/ziggurat_envelope.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The positive density kernel and every sixteenth proposal rectangle. Selected rectangles are separated by omitted strips. The base is drawn at its physical width r, with the normal tail continuing to its right.)
\alttext(A decreasing normal density curve with a selection of equal-area horizontal proposal rectangles. Rectangles protrude slightly above the curve at their right edges.)

>> Why almost every draw takes the fast path

Choose \(I\) uniformly from \(0,\ldots,N-1\), independently draw
\(U\) uniformly from \((-1,1)\), and set \(X=Ux_I\). If
\(|U|<x_{I+1}/x_I\), the point is in a region known to lie under the
curve, so it can be accepted without evaluating an exponential. The
per-proposal probability of this shortcut is

$$
p_{\rm fast}=\frac1N\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\frac{x_{i+1}}{x_i},\qquad
p_{\rm accept}=\frac{\sqrt{\pi/2}}{NV}.
$$
\alttext(The fast-path probability is the average inner-to-outer width ratio. Overall proposal acceptance is the positive normal-kernel area divided by the total envelope area N V.)

These probabilities use proposals as the denominator. The fraction of
returned samples that took the fast path is instead
\(p_{\rm fast}/p_{\rm accept}\). Rejected proposals restart the outer
loop, so confusing the two denominators overstates or understates the
shortcut rate.

If \(I>0\) and the shortcut fails, sample a height uniformly between the
two strip boundaries and accept only if it lies below \(f(X)\). Dividing
the height comparison by \(f(X)\) gives the implementation's test

$$
a_1+W(a_0-a_1)<1,\quad
a_0=e^{-(x_I^2-X^2)/2},\quad
a_1=e^{-(x_{I+1}^2-X^2)/2},\quad W\sim U(0,1).
$$
\alttext(The wedge accepts when a uniformly interpolated boundary-height ratio is below one, with each ratio scaled by the kernel at the proposed coordinate.)

This samples uniformly under the curve within the strip. Equal proposal
areas make uniform layer selection correct. Repeating after rejection
leaves exactly the target normal density. Construction and storage cost
\(O(N)\); each sample takes expected \(O(1)\) time, with no finite
worst-case rejection count. This implementation uses separate engine draws
for the coordinate and index, following Doornik's remedy for shared-bit
selection dependencies; it does not claim a universal speed advantage.

>> The base layer and exponential tail

A base proposal inside \(r\) is accepted by the shortcut. The remaining
base mass selects the normal tail. Set \(X=r+E\), propose
\(E\sim\operatorname{Exp}(r)\), and note

$$
f(r+e)=f(r)e^{-re-e^2/2}.
$$
\alttext(The normal kernel at r plus e factors into the kernel at r, an exponential decay with rate r, and an additional exponential of minus e squared over two.)

Relative to the exponential proposal, the remaining acceptance factor is
\(e^{-e^2/2}\). If \(Y\sim\operatorname{Exp}(1)\), accepting when
\(2Y\ge E^2\) has exactly that probability. Restore the original sign.
The code forms exponential draws with `-log1p(-u)` for
\(u\in[0,1)\), avoiding the undefined endpoint in `-log(u)` when a
finite-precision uniform source returns zero.

> Checking the sampler, including the uncommon paths

Actual counters should obey \(\text{fast}+\text{wedge}+\text{tail}=n\)
and \(\text{proposals}=n+\text{rejected}\). Tail proposals have their
own inner retry loop and are counted separately. A million returns makes
the rare tail branch visible for both table sizes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  for (std::size_t n : {128, 256})
    std::print("{}\n", dm::algorithms::ZigguratNormal(n).diagnostics(1000000).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| layers | draws | proposals | fast | wedge | tail | rejected | tail_trials | fast_per_proposal | expected_fast_per_proposal | expected_acceptance |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 128 | 1000000 | 1012478 | 984504 | 14926 | 570 | 12478 | 609 | 0.972371 | 0.972440 | 0.987789 |

| layers | draws | proposals | fast | wedge | tail | rejected | tail_trials | fast_per_proposal | expected_fast_per_proposal | expected_acceptance |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 256 | 1000000 | 1006847 | 991783 | 7947 | 270 | 6847 | 290 | 0.985038 | 0.985081 | 0.993322 |
// result_end
\caption(Instrumented path counts and theoretical probabilities. Outer rejections and inner tail retries are different events.)

The 128-layer run uses 1 012 478 outer proposals for a million returned
values; the 256-layer run uses 1 006 847. Their observed fast-per-proposal
fractions, 0.972371 and 0.985038, agree with the width-ratio predictions.
Both runs exercise hundreds of tail returns, including inner retries.

For a standard normal sample, the sample mean has standard error
\(1/\sqrt n\), the sample variance has standard error approximately
\(\sqrt{2/(n-1)}\), and the empirical two-sided tail beyond \(t\)
has standard error \(\sqrt{p_t(1-p_t)/n}\), where
\(p_t=\operatorname{erfc}(t/\sqrt2)\). The table reports Monte Carlo
checks and a KS comparison against the fully specified \(N(0,1)\),
without fitting its parameters to the generated sample.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ziggurat_qq.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| layers | n | mean | sample sd | fraction beyond 3.5 | exact tail | tail MC SE | KS D | KS p |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t n : {128, 256}) {
    const auto z = dm::algorithms::ZigguratNormal(n).draw(1000000);
    const double p = std::erfc(3.5 / std::sqrt(2.0));
    const auto tail = std::count_if(z.begin(), z.end(), [](double v) { return std::abs(v) > 3.5; });
    const auto ks = dm::stats::ks_test_one_sample_normal(z);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.4f} |\n",
        n, z.size(), dm::mean(z), dm::sd(z), double(tail) / z.size(), p,
        std::sqrt(p * (1-p) / z.size()), ks.statistic, ks.p_value);
  }
  const auto display = dm::algorithms::ZigguratNormal().draw(3000);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::qqnorm(display).abline(0, 1, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1)
      .title("Ziggurat draws against the specified standard normal")
      .size(800, 500).save_svg("build/ziggurat_qq.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| layers | n | mean | sample sd | fraction beyond 3.5 | exact tail | tail MC SE | KS D | KS p |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 128 | 1000000 | 0.000921 | 1.001819 | 0.000460 | 0.000465 | 0.000022 | 0.001208 | 0.1082 |
| 256 | 1000000 | 0.000129 | 1.001558 | 0.000496 | 0.000465 | 0.000022 | 0.000886 | 0.4125 |
\image(figures/ziggurat_qq.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Moments, an explicit tail check and KS statistics for a million draws. The Q-Q plot uses a separate 3000-draw display and the identity line, rather than a line fitted to the sample.)
\alttext(A normal quantile plot of generated values against theoretical standard-normal quantiles, with an identity reference line.)

Both sample means are within one theoretical standard error of zero.
The observed standard deviations are about 1.002, and the two measured
3.5-sigma tail fractions are within two Monte Carlo standard errors of
the exact value. An independent R calculation on the same saved draws
reproduces the means, standard deviations, tail counts and KS statistics.
R's asymptotic KS p-values differ slightly because this library applies
the Stephens finite-sample correction.

> Applying it to the schools

We condition on each reported effect \(y_j\) as a hypothetical mean and
use its reported standard error \(s_j\) as the scale of a new
measurement. Under this plug-in Gaussian model,

$$
Y_j^*=y_j+s_j Z,\qquad
\mathbb E[Y_j^*]=y_j,\qquad
\operatorname{sd}(Y_j^*)=s_j,\qquad
\Pr(Y_j^*>0)=\Phi(y_j/s_j).
$$
\alttext(A replicate equals the reported effect plus its standard error times a standard normal draw. Its mean and standard deviation equal those inputs, and its probability of being positive is the normal CDF of their ratio.)

This provides a direct oracle for a useful simulation. It does not mean
that \(\Phi(y_j/s_j)\) is the probability a fixed unknown effect is
positive; that interpretation requires a specified inferential model.
The library preserves the original dataset columns and adds simulated
means, standard deviations and positive fractions.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ziggurat_probabilities.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto simulation = dm::algorithms::ZigguratNormal().simulate_measurements(
      dm::datasets::eight_schools(), "effect", "std_error", 200000);
  std::print("{}\n", simulation.to_md(5));
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .points(simulation.get_column("normal_positive").values(), simulation.get_column("mc_positive").values(),
              "school replicate", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5)
      .abline(0, 1, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1)
      .title("Positive measurement replicates: simulation and exact model")
      .x_label("exact normal probability").y_label("simulated positive fraction")
      .x_limits(0.3, 1).y_limits(0.3, 1).size(800, 500).save_svg("build/ziggurat_probabilities.svg");
}
```
