\image(figures/ziggurat_probabilities.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Simulated measurement summaries, exact positive probabilities and their theoretical Monte Carlo standard errors. The plot compares positive fractions to the exact model for all eight schools.)
\alttext(Eight simulated positive fractions plotted against exact normal probabilities, close to the identity line.)

`mc_positive_se` measures simulation error only. Increasing the number of
draws by four halves this error, but it does not reduce any school's
reported standard error. More simulation cannot create more observations.

> Conclusion

The equal-area construction explains uniform layer selection, and the
width ratios explain the cheap acceptance path. Explicit counters check
both the common shortcut and the rare tail branch. Gaussian measurement
replicates give exact distributional oracles on a real dataset, while the
separate Monte Carlo standard errors show what simulation accuracy means.
A sampler test can reveal implementation mistakes; passing a few moment,
tail and KS checks does not certify all possible serial or high-dimensional
properties of a random stream.

> References

- G. Marsaglia and W. W. Tsang (2000). *The Ziggurat Method for Generating Random Variables*.
  Journal of Statistical Software 5(8). https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v005.i08
- J. A. Doornik (2005). *An Improved Ziggurat Method to Generate Normal Random Samples*.
  https://doornik.com/research/ziggurat.pdf
